---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ClosingCostTool from '../../components/ClosingCostTool.astro';
import RelatedTools from '@kit/components/RelatedTools.astro';
import { SITE, toolBySlug, toolsExcept } from '../../lib/site';
import { STATES } from '@data/states';

const tool = toolBySlug('closing-cost-calculator')!;
const canonical = `/tools/${tool.slug}`;
const title = `Closing Cost Calculator — Full Line-by-Line Estimate | ${SITE.name}`;
const description =
  'Estimate buyer closing costs broken into lender, title, government and ' +
  'prepaid items, with transfer tax and recording fees for your state.';

const schema = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE.url },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Calculators', item: `${SITE.url}/tools` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: tool.nav, item: `${SITE.url}${canonical}` },
  ],
}];

const related = toolsExcept(tool.slug).slice(0, 3).map((t) => ({
  href: `/tools/${t.slug}`, title: t.title, desc: t.blurb,
}));
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} {canonical} {schema}>
  <ClosingCostTool
    state={null}
    title="Closing Cost Calculator"
    intro="Every line a buyer pays at closing, grouped the way a Loan Estimate groups them — so you can see which items you are allowed to shop for and which you are not."
    breadcrumbs={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { href: '/tools', label: 'Calculators' }, { label: tool.nav }]}
  >
    <section style="margin-top:var(--s-7)">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Closing costs by state</h2>
        <p>Transfer taxes and recording fees are set locally and vary enormously — from nothing at all in fourteen states to 4% of the price in Delaware. Pick your state for local figures.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chip-row">
        {STATES.map((st) => <a class="chip" href={`/closing-costs/${st.slug}`}>{st.name}</a>)}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="prose" style="margin-top:var(--s-7)">
      <h2>What closing costs actually are</h2>
      <p>
        Closing costs are every charge that lands between an accepted offer and the
        keys, excluding the down payment itself. For a buyer they typically run 2%
        to 5% of the purchase price — a range wide enough to be nearly useless,
        which is why the breakdown above matters more than the total.
      </p>
      <p>
        The federal Loan Estimate splits these into sections for a reason, and the
        distinction is the single most useful thing to understand: some charges are
        fixed by the lender or the government, and some are services you may choose
        a provider for. Only the second group is negotiable, and it is usually worth
        $1,000 to $2,500.
      </p>

      <h2>The four buckets</h2>
      <h3>Lender fees</h3>
      <p>
        Origination, underwriting, processing, appraisal, credit report. These are
        set by the lender, and you cannot negotiate them down within a single
        lender — but they vary a great deal between lenders. This is why the useful
        move is to collect three Loan Estimates and compare section A across them,
        rather than arguing with one loan officer about a $200 processing fee.
      </p>
      <h3>Title and settlement</h3>
      <p>
        The lender's title policy is mandatory. The owner's policy is optional and
        you should almost always buy it — a one-time premium that protects your
        ownership against defects, forged deeds and missed heirs for as long as you
        own the property. It is also the largest genuinely shoppable line on the
        statement. In most states you may choose your own title company; in a few,
        premiums are state-regulated and identical everywhere, in which case shop
        the settlement fee instead.
      </p>
      <h3>Government charges</h3>
      <p>
        Recording fees and transfer taxes. Fixed by statute, entirely non-negotiable,
        and the reason the same house costs thousands more to buy in one state than
        another. Fourteen states impose no transfer tax at all; Delaware charges 4%.
        Who customarily pays is convention rather than law, and it is genuinely
        negotiable in a slow market.
      </p>
      <h3>Prepaid items and escrow</h3>
      <p>
        These are not fees. Prepaid interest covers the days between closing and the
        end of the month. The escrow deposit funds an account that pays your taxes
        and insurance when they come due — it is your money, held on your behalf,
        and refunded if you sell or refinance. Treat it as a cash flow requirement,
        not a cost, when you compare lenders.
      </p>

      <h2>How to actually reduce them</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Shop the title company.</strong> The largest single controllable line. Quotes for identical coverage routinely differ by $800 or more.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask for a reissue rate.</strong> If the property changed hands in the last ten years, the owner's policy often qualifies for a discount of 20–40%. It is rarely offered unprompted.</li>
        <li><strong>Negotiate seller credits.</strong> In a buyer's market, asking the seller to cover 2–3% toward closing costs is routine and often easier than a price reduction, since it costs the seller the same and helps you more.</li>
        <li><strong>Close near the end of the month.</strong> Prepaid interest is charged daily to month end. Closing on the 28th instead of the 3rd saves most of a month's interest — though your first full payment then arrives sooner.</li>
        <li><strong>Compare Loan Estimates on the same day.</strong> Rates and credits move; quotes from different weeks are not comparable.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>The lender cannot surprise you.</strong> Charges in section A of the
        Loan Estimate may not increase at all between estimate and closing, and
        section C items may not rise more than 10% in aggregate if you use a
        provider from the lender's written list. If the Closing Disclosure exceeds
        those tolerances, the lender owes you the difference. Compare the two
        documents line by line before you sign.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="prose">
      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <dl class="faq">
        <dt>Can closing costs be rolled into the loan?</dt>
        <dd>
          On a purchase, generally no — that is what seller credits are for. On a
          refinance, yes, though you then pay interest on them for the life of the
          loan. The <a href="/tools/refinance-break-even-calculator">refinance
          break-even calculator</a> models that directly.
        </dd>
        <dt>Do I pay closing costs if I pay cash?</dt>
        <dd>
          Yes, but far less. Everything in the lender bucket disappears, along with
          prepaid interest and escrow. You still owe title, settlement, recording and
          any transfer tax — typically about 1% of the price rather than 3%.
        </dd>
        <dt>Why is my estimate higher than this calculator?</dt>
        <dd>
          Usually HOA transfer fees, a survey requirement, a second appraisal, or
          local charges that exist in one county and nowhere else. Compare against
          your actual Loan Estimate and treat any line you do not recognise as a
          question for the lender.
        </dd>
        <dt>Who chooses the title company?</dt>
        <dd>
          You do, in most states, and federal law prohibits a seller from requiring
          you to use a particular one as a condition of sale. Lenders provide a list
          of providers they will accept; using someone off that list is allowed but
          removes the 10% tolerance protection.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <RelatedTools items={related} />
  </ClosingCostTool>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/cc-island';
</script>
